### Part (a) — Use of the `goto` Statement in C

- **Purpose:** `goto label;` transfers execution unconditionally to `label:` within the **same function** (jumping across two different functions with `goto` is illegal in C; variable-length arrays cannot be jumped into).
- **Practical Use Case:** While unrestricted `goto` is discouraged in structured programming because backward jumps obscure invariants, forward `goto` provides a clean single-step exit out of **deeply nested loops** (where `break;` exits only one level) or jumps to shared error/resource cleanup:

```c
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < 10; j++) {
        if (grid[i][j] < 0) goto error_exit;
    }
}
error_exit:
printf("Stopped on negative entry.\n");
```

### Part (b) — Nested-Loop C Program to Print `5 / 45 / 345 / 2345 / 12345`

- **Row/Column Pattern Analysis:** Across `5` rows, the starting digit `start` counts **down** from `5` to `1`, while inner column variable `j` counts **up** from `start` to `5` on each row:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int start, j;
    for (start = 5; start >= 1; start--) {
        for (j = start; j <= 5; j++) {
            printf("%d", j);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

- **Row-by-Row Trace & Verified Output:**

| Outer `start` | Inner `j` values (`start..5`) | Printed Line |
|---|---|---|
| `5` | `5` | `5` |
| `4` | `4, 5` | `45` |
| `3` | `3, 4, 5` | `345` |
| `2` | `2, 3, 4, 5` | `2345` |
| `1` | `1, 2, 3, 4, 5` | `12345` |

> [!MARKS] 5 + 3 = 8 Marks (`a`: C program to check whether a matrix is an identity matrix — 5m · `b`: Array definition & advantages — 3m)

### Part (a) — C Program to Check Whether a Matrix Is an Identity Matrix

- **Identity Matrix Definition (`Iₙ`):** A **square** `n x n` matrix in which every principal diagonal entry equals `1` (`a[i][j] == 1` when `i == j`) and every off-diagonal entry equals `0` (`a[i][j] == 0` when `i != j`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[10][10], r, c, i, j, is_identity = 1;

    printf("Enter rows and columns (max 10 10): ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &r, &c) != 2 || r < 1 || r > 10 || c < 1 || c > 10)
        return 1;

    if (r != c) {
        printf("Not an Identity Matrix (must be square).\n");
        return 0;
    }

    printf("Enter %d elements:\n", r * c);
    for (i = 0; i < r; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < c; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &a[i][j]);
            if ((i == j && a[i][j] != 1) || (i != j && a[i][j] != 0)) {
                is_identity = 0;
            }
        }
    }

    if (is_identity)
        printf("The matrix is an Identity Matrix.\n");
    else
        printf("The matrix is NOT an Identity Matrix.\n");
    return 0;
}
```

### Part (b) — Definition and Advantages of an Array

- **Definition:** An **array** is a linear, fixed-size data structure storing a collection of **homogeneous** (same-type) elements in **contiguous memory locations** under a single variable name.
- **Key Advantages:**
  1. **`O(1)` Random Access:** Any element `a[i]` is reached directly via `base + i * sizeof(type)` without sequential scanning.
  2. **Compact Loop Processing:** One `for` loop processes hundreds of values instead of declaring hundreds of separate variables.
  3. **Cache Locality & Foundation:** Contiguous layout maximizes CPU cache hits and underpins matrices, strings, heaps, and hash tables.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: `strcmp()` and `strcat()` — 3m · `b`: C program to toggle lowercase/uppercase without `<string.h>` — 5m)

### Part (a) — String Operations `strcmp()` and `strcat()` (`<string.h>`)

- **`int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2)`:**
  - Compares `s1` and `s2` character-by-character using unsigned ASCII values until a mismatch or `'\0'`.
  - Returns `0` if `s1` and `s2` are identical, `< 0` if `s1 < s2` (e.g., `strcmp("ABC", "abc") < 0`), and `> 0` if `s1 > s2`.
- **`char *strcat(char *dest, const char *src)`:**
  - Appends a copy of `src` onto the end of `dest` (overwriting `dest`'s `'\0'` and adding a new terminating `'\0'`); `dest` must have capacity `>= strlen(dest) + strlen(src) + 1`.

### Part (b) — C Program to Toggle Lowercase ↔ Uppercase Without `<string.h>`

- **ASCII Case-Offset Rule:** Uppercase `'A'..'Z'` (`65..90`) and lowercase `'a'..'z'` (`97..122`) differ by **`32`** (`'a' - 'A' == 32`): subtract `32` (`ch - 'a' + 'A'`) for lowercase→uppercase, and add `32` (`ch - 'A' + 'a'`) for uppercase→lowercase, stopping at `'\0'`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char str[100];
    int i;

    printf("Enter a string: ");
    if (fgets(str, sizeof(str), stdin) == NULL) return 1;

    for (i = 0; str[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (str[i] >= 'a' && str[i] <= 'z') {
            str[i] = (char)(str[i] - ('a' - 'A'));      /* to uppercase */
        } else if (str[i] >= 'A' && str[i] <= 'Z') {
            str[i] = (char)(str[i] + ('a' - 'A'));      /* to lowercase */
        }
    }

    printf("Converted string: %s", str);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Output (`"Hello C 2023!"`):** Prints `Converted string: hELLO c 2023!` — digits, spaces, and punctuation fail both range checks and remain untouched, `fgets()` safely prevents buffer overflow while reading multi-word lines, and zero `<string.h>` functions are used.

> [!MARKS] 5 + 3 = 8 Marks (`a`: C program to add two numbers using pointers and function — 5m · `b`: Pointer definition & advantages — 3m)

### Part (a) — C Program to Add Two Numbers Using Pointers and a Function

- **Design:** Pass addresses `&x`, `&y`, and `&sum` to `void addNumbers(const int *a, const int *b, int *result)` so the function reads `*a` and `*b` and writes their sum directly into `*result`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void addNumbers(const int *a, const int *b, int *result) {
    *result = *a + *b;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y, sum;
    printf("Enter two integers: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &x, &y) != 2) return 1;

    addNumbers(&x, &y, &sum);           /* pass addresses */
    printf("Sum of %d and %d = %d\n", x, y, sum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Run (`x = 27, y = 15`):** `addNumbers` dereferences `*a` (`27`) and `*b` (`15`), writes `42` into `*result`, and `main()` prints `Sum of 27 and 15 = 42`.

### Part (b) — What Is a Pointer? Advantages of Using Pointers in C

- **Definition:** A **pointer** is a derived variable that holds the **memory address** of another typed variable (`int n = 10; int *p = &n;` where `*p == 10`).
- **Key Advantages:**
  1. **Call-by-Address Mutation:** Enables functions to modify caller variables and return multiple outputs.
  2. **Dynamic Heap Memory:** Required for `malloc()`, `calloc()`, `realloc()`, and `free()`.
  3. **Zero-Copy Array/Struct Passing:** Passes large arrays and `struct` objects efficiently via a single address instead of copying kilobytes on the stack.
  4. **Dynamic Data Structures:** Essential for building linked lists, trees, and graphs.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: C program to print ASCII value of a character — 3m · `b`: C program to print prime numbers in an interval using functions — 5m)

### Part (a) — C Program to Find the ASCII Value of a Character

- **Concept:** A `char` stores a 1-byte integer character code; printing it with `%c` displays the glyph while `%d` prints its decimal ASCII code (`'A' -> 65`, `'a' -> 97`, `'0' -> 48`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char ch;
    printf("Enter a character: ");
    if (scanf(" %c", &ch) != 1) return 1;
    printf("ASCII value of '%c' = %d\n", ch, (unsigned char)ch);
    return 0;
}
```

### Part (b) — C Program to Find All Prime Numbers in an Interval Using Functions

- **Trial-Division Primality (`O(√n)`):** An integer `n >= 2` is prime iff it has no divisor `d` satisfying `2 <= d * d <= n` (`0`, `1`, and negatives are not prime).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int isPrime(int n) {
    int d;
    if (n < 2) return 0;
    for (d = 2; d * d <= n; d++) {
        if (n % d == 0) return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

void printPrimes(int low, int high) {
    int num;
    printf("Prime numbers between %d and %d: ", low, high);
    for (num = low; num <= high; num++) {
        if (isPrime(num)) {
            printf("%d ", num);
        }
    }
    printf("\n");
}

int main(void) {
    int low, high;
    printf("Enter lower and upper bounds: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &low, &high) != 2) return 1;
    if (low > high) { int t = low; low = high; high = t; }
    printPrimes(low, high);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Output (`10 30`):** `Prime numbers between 10 and 30: 11 13 17 19 23 29`.

> [!MARKS] 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8 Marks (`a`: `malloc()` — 2m · `b`: `calloc()` — 2m · `c`: `realloc()` — 2m · `d`: `free()` — 2m)

All four dynamic heap-management functions are declared in `<stdlib.h>`:

### (a) `malloc()` — Memory Allocation
- **Prototype:** `void *malloc(size_t size);`
- **Behaviour:** Allocates **one contiguous block** of `size` uninitialized bytes on the heap (contents are indeterminate garbage) and returns a generic pointer to the first byte, or `NULL` if heap allocation fails.

### (b) `calloc()` — Contiguous Allocation
- **Prototype:** `void *calloc(size_t n, size_t elem_size);`
- **Behaviour:** Allocates heap memory for an array of `n` elements of `elem_size` bytes each (`n * elem_size` total) and **initializes every byte to `0`**, returning `NULL` on failure.

### (c) `realloc()` — Re-Allocation
- **Prototype:** `void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t new_size);`
- **Behaviour:** Resizes a previously allocated heap block pointed to by `ptr` to `new_size` bytes (either expanding in place or allocating a new block, copying up to `min(old_size, new_size)` bytes, and freeing the old block); if it fails, it returns `NULL` and leaves the original block untouched.

### (d) `free()` — Deallocation
- **Prototype:** `void free(void *ptr);`
- **Behaviour:** Releases a heap block obtained from `malloc`/`calloc`/`realloc` back to the free store to prevent **memory leaks** (`free(NULL)` is a safe no-op; set `ptr = NULL;` afterward to prevent dangling/double-free bugs).

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    int *a = calloc(3, sizeof *a);          /* {0, 0, 0} */
    if (!a) return 1;
    int *tmp = realloc(a, 5 * sizeof *a);   /* grow to 5 ints */
    if (!tmp) { free(a); return 1; }
    a = tmp;
    a[3] = 40; a[4] = 50;
    printf("%d %d\n", a[0], a[4]);          /* 0 50 */
    free(a);
    a = NULL;
    return 0;
}
```
